# thermia2mqtt

> [!NOTE]
> This project is 100% hallucinated and carefully reviewed by yours truly, including this README!
> The tool works on my hardware. Will it work on yours? I don't know! You are welcome to submit
> fixes!

Exposes a Thermia Genesis platform heat pump to MQTT following the
[Homie 5 convention](https://homieiot.github.io/). Talks to the heat pump over Modbus TCP.

Register definitions come from Thermia's Modbus protocol guides for the Genesis platform.

## Usage

```
thermia2mqtt mqtt --tcp heatpump.local --mqtt-broker mqtt://broker:1883?client_id=thermia
thermia2mqtt registers "tap water"
```

By default **nothing is ever written** to the heat pump: all the properties are read-only and no
`/set` topics are subscribed to. Pass `--allow-writes` to be able to change the settings (such as
the tap water start and stop temperatures) from MQTT.

### Choosing the nodes

The properties are grouped into nodes. Only the nodes that every installation has are enabled by
default: `alarms,system,heat-pump,heating,additional-heater,tap-water`. `--nodes` takes a
comma-separated list and **replaces** that default, so name all the nodes you want, defaults
included:

```
thermia2mqtt mqtt --tcp heatpump.local --nodes alarms,system,heat-pump,heating,tap-water,power,statistics
```

Leaving a node out of the list disables it. The nodes that are not enabled by default are:
`mix-valve-1` to `mix-valve-5`, `cooling`, `pool`, `twc`, `wcs`, `hgw`, `hot-gas-pump`,
`surplus-heat`, `secondary-units`, `power`, `power-limit`, `statistics` (these use registers that
Thermia does not document), `immersion-heater` and `electric-meter`. Many of these need
accessories or a newer firmware, and the heat pump has nothing at some of their registers. Unknown
node names are an error. Properties whose registers the heat pump has nothing at are left out of the
device description.

`THERMIA2MQTT_LOG=debug` makes it more talkative. At startup it logs how many
requests one polling cycle takes. The heat pump takes about half a second to answer any request. Each
connection to it waits for the response before sending the next request, but the connections are
served independently, so `--connections` (4 by default) is what makes polling fast. `--max-read-count`
makes for fewer, larger requests.

## Caveats

* The guide does not say which registers are signed. Temperatures and a few others are treated as
  signed (see `is_signed` in the generator.)
* Not every heat pump has every register. If the heat pump rejects a request a warning is logged
  and the registers are left unpublished, which, as per the Homie convention, holds up the device
  from becoming ready.
